╭─────────────────────────────── Round 1 · CRITIC (Conf: 0.72, Ready: False) ────────────────────────────────╮
│ • [C1 -> A1] (CHALLENGED): A single global 90-day threshold treats Norway and a country with one biannual  │
│ ministerial cycle identically. Countries with seasonal or low-frequency diplomatic rhythms (parliamentary  │
│ recess, elections, summit-driven cadence) will be flagged cold every cycle while being perfectly healthy.  │
│ Regional Coordinators will quickly learn these are noise and start ignoring the digest, so the one         │
│ genuinely cold high-priority country gets lost in the same pile. The 25% day-one cap still allows ~25      │
│ flagged countries, which is already a fatigue-level volume for a coordinator portfolio of perhaps 10-15    │
│ countries. Whether 90 days is the right definition of cold is a policy question, but there must be a       │
│ bounded engineering mitigation, such as a coordinator-visible reason or an exclusion for countries in an   │
│ active mission.                                                                                            │
│   Eval: Proposer explicitly scopes out per-country thresholds and defers the definition of cold to humans, │
│ but ships a hardcoded global default with no mechanism to avoid flagging countries with active missions or │
│ known low-cadence relationships. This leaves a real false-positive risk unaddressed.                       │
│ • [C2 -> A2] (CHALLENGED): The signal is last logged Engagement, but logging is voluntary and uneven. A    │
│ coordinator who is in weekly phone contact with a ministry but has not logged it will trigger a false      │
│ 'cold' alert, while a country where an intern bulk-logged a mass-email Engagement looks warm. Turnover     │
│ compounds this: when a coordinator leaves or a country is reassigned, the backlog of unlogged contact      │
│ makes the countries look cold exactly when handover is weakest. Also, 'any Engagement of any type' lets    │
│ low-value records (automated or mass-distribution mailings, a logged circular) reset the clock, hiding a   │
│ truly cold relationship. The 'complete enough' assumption is untested; the day-one back-test only measures │
│ how many are flagged, not whether those flags are true.                                                    │
│   Eval: A2 is stated as an assumption with no validation. The back-test success criterion checks only the  │
│ volume of flags and not their accuracy, so the risk of false positives and false negatives from poor       │
│ logging data is unmitigated.                                                                               │
│ • [C3 -> A3] (CHALLENGED): Assumes every country has a current, correct Regional Coordinator. With         │
│ turnover across 100 countries, some countries will have no coordinator, a departed one (account            │
│ deactivated), or a stale assignment. The alert then goes to nobody or to the wrong person, and the country │
│ silently stays cold with no fallback; the exact failure the feature exists to prevent. In the other        │
│ direction, a former coordinator who still holds Country access could keep receiving emails with            │
│ last-contact names for a country they no longer work on. The proposal has no handling for unassigned or    │
│ inactive recipients.                                                                                       │
│   Eval: A3 asserts the assignment exists but provides no fallback or detection when it does not, and no    │
│ check that the recipient is still active and authorized at send time.                                      │
│ • [C4 -> A3] (CHALLENGED): The alert and the weekly email include the name of the last contact engaged.    │
│ Email digests leave the CRM's access-controlled boundary: they sit in mailboxes, are forwarded, are synced │
│ to phones, and persist after a user's CRM access is revoked. 'Inherits Country/Mission access' only holds  │
│ at render time in-app, not for an email sent at 06:00 that is read later. Contact names tied to a country  │
│ and a 'relationship cold' label are diplomatically sensitive. Also, Engagements may be restricted by       │
│ mission or classification, so if last_engagement_date or last contact is derived from a restricted         │
│ Engagement, the alert can leak the existence and the contact of an engagement the recipient cannot see in  │
│ the CRM. The nightly job computes across all engagements, so it is unclear whether it runs with the        │
│ recipient's permissions.                                                                                   │
│   Eval: The proposal treats the email digest as equivalent to in-app visibility. It does not state whether │
│ the derived fields are filtered per recipient or whether contact names appear in email bodies, so          │
│ confidentiality leakage via email and restricted-Engagement derivation is unaddressed.                     │
│ • [C5 -> A1] (CHALLENGED): The go-live behavior and the transition semantics are ill-defined. Countries    │
│ with no Engagement history are flagged once at go-live; for a CRM that has not been logging engagement     │
│ consistently, this can dump a large batch of labeled-cold alerts onto coordinators on day one, and         │
│ 'flagged once' then never re-evaluates them if they are never engaged. Also, 'cold' is an edge-triggered   │
│ notification, and the re-reminder is only through the digest every 30 days. A job failure or a skipped     │
│ nightly run could silently miss the crossing, and there is no detection of job failure. The 'exactly one   │
│ notification' criterion is untestable without idempotency and missed-run handling.                         │
│   Eval: The Proposer offers no idempotency, missed-run catch-up, or monitoring for the nightly job, and    │
│ the day-one handling of countries with no history is internally inconsistent with the 'no more than 25%    │
│ flagged' criterion.                                                                                        │
│                                                                                                            │
│ Disagreement Score: 0.88 | Gate Status: ROUND_1_MINIMUM_LOCK                                               │
