│ • [C1 -> A1] (ACCEPTED): Single global 90-day threshold flags countries with seasonal or low-frequency     │
│ rhythms and creates alert fatigue for coordinators.                                                        │
│   Eval: The Proposer adopted the bounded mitigations I asked for: suppression while a country has an       │
│ active Mission, a plain reason line showing days-since and threshold, and a day-one 25% cap with a         │
│ coordinator sample check. The remaining per-country false positives are an acceptable MVP risk. Whether 90 │
│ days defines cold is flagged as a human policy decision, and post-launch feedback is named as the trigger  │
│ for revisiting it.                                                                                         │
│ • [C2 -> A2] (CHALLENGED): Unlogged contact makes countries look falsely cold. Low-value or                │
│ mass-distribution Engagements reset the clock and hide a genuinely cold relationship, for example an       │
│ intern bulk-logging a circular for a country with no real contact for six months.                          │
│   Eval: The defense covers false positives well. A false cold alert is cheap to check, clears itself when  │
│ the contact is logged, and acts as a handover prompt. The new release gate samples 10 flagged countries.   │
│ It does not address false negatives. A mass mailing that resets the clock produces no alert, so no         │
│ coordinator ever looks at the country history the Proposer relies on. The gate only measures precision on  │
│ flagged countries and says nothing about cold countries that were missed. A cheap fix exists with no new   │
│ data entry: extend the release gate to also have coordinators review a sample of about 10 unflagged        │
│ countries whose most recent Engagement is a bulk or multi-recipient record, and record the result. The     │
│ Proposer should state what happens if that sample shows masking, for example the threshold or type         │
│ filtering goes back to humans before release.                                                              │
│ • [C3 -> A3] (ACCEPTED): Unassigned, departed, or stale coordinator assignments send the alert to nobody   │
│ or to a former coordinator, so the country stays cold with no one aware.                                   │
│   Eval: Recipients are now resolved at send time and restricted to active accounts that still hold Country │
│ access. A fallback to Operations-role holders with access covers the nobody case. A criterion tests that   │
│ 100% of cold countries resolve to at least one recipient. No new table or screen is needed. Choosing the   │
│ fallback role is flagged for human confirmation, and that is acceptable for an MVP.                        │
│ • [C4 -> A3] (ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN): Email digests leave the access-controlled boundary. Restricted          │
│ Engagements could leak their existence or date to recipients who cannot see them.                          │
│   Eval: Most of the engineering risk is closed. The email carries only country name, a days-since band,    │
│ and a link. Contact names and engagement dates are removed, and a test on rendered output is added. Two    │
│ points remain that engineers cannot settle. First, whether a country name with a 'cold' label may appear   │
│ in email is a data-classification decision. Second, the system job counts restricted Engagements toward    │
│ the clock, so a days-since count can implicitly reflect the recency of an Engagement the recipient cannot  │
│ see. Whether that is acceptable depends on classification and clearance authority. The Proposer has        │
│ already stated the link-only fallback if humans disallow the label.                                        │
│ • [C5 -> A1] (ACCEPTED): Go-live flood from no-history countries, edge-triggered misses after a skipped    │
│ run, and no detection of job failure.                                                                      │
│   Eval: The move to level-triggered with a single auto-filled last_alerted_at column makes the job         │
│ idempotent and self-healing after a skipped run. No-history countries are re-evaluated every 30 days and   │
│ counted in the 25% day-one cap. Job failures go to existing platform alerting. Double-run and skipped-run  │
│ tests are added. This is sufficient for an MVP.                                                            │
│                                                                                                            │
