────────────────────────────────────────── Final Decision Document ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberation Decision Document
**Feature Request:** The CRM should alert us when our relationship with a country goes cold.

**Termination Reason:** `CONVERGENCE_ALL_ISSUES_RESOLVED_OR_ESCALATED (CRITIC_SIGNALED_CONVERGENCE)` | **Final
Disagreement Score:** `0.0` | **Confidence (Proposer / Critic):** `0.72` / `0.30`

## Executive Summary
V1 runs a nightly job that marks a country cold when no Engagement has been logged for more than 90 days (one 
global threshold) and the country has no active Mission. Each cold country gets one in-app notification per 
30-day window. The job is idempotent and self-heals after a skipped night, and alerts auto-clear within 24 
hours of a new Engagement. Coordinators enter nothing. The main trade-off was simplicity against accuracy: the
Critic pressed on false positives from a single threshold and uneven logging, and on false negatives from 
bulk-logged Engagements. These were bounded by active-mission suppression, a reason line, a day-one cap of 
25%, and a one-off two-part release gate, instead of per-country settings or Engagement-type filtering. The 
agents agreed on the engineering scope but could not settle the sensitivity of alert content. Three scope 
items are pending a human decision on C4: the in-app notification content, the weekly email digest, and 
send-time recipient resolution with its fallback role. Humans must decide whether a country name with a 'cold'
label may appear in email and whether restricted Engagements may count toward the days-since figure. They must
also confirm the 3-of-10 release-hold cutoff.

## 1. Agreed Scope
### Included in Scope (v1 MVP)
- Nightly job computes last_engagement_date per Country as the most recent existing Engagement linked to any 
Contact, Mission, or Project of that country. The job runs with system permissions and counts restricted 
Engagements toward 'warm', because they can only suppress an alert and never reveal anything. It is derived on
the fly with no new entity or manual field.
- A country is cold when days since last_engagement_date exceed one global threshold (default 90 days, one 
system config value, no per-country settings) AND the country has no active Mission. Active-mission countries 
are suppressed. Countries with no Engagement history are treated as cold with a 'no recorded engagement' label
and counted in the day-one cap.
- Level-triggered state instead of edge-triggered: one auto-filled column, Country.last_alerted_at, written by
the job. Each night a cold country with no alert in the last 30 days gets one in-app notification. Reruns are 
idempotent and a skipped night self-heals on the next run. Nothing is entered by coordinators.
- Alert auto-clears within 24 hours of a new Engagement being logged for the country. No acknowledge, snooze, 
or dismiss action is required of the coordinator.
- Nightly job failures are written to the existing platform job logging and alerting for engineering. No new 
screen or queue is added.
- One-off pre-release release gate, not a product feature: a single query over existing Engagement data lists 
unflagged countries whose most recent Engagement is a bulk or multi-recipient record. Coordinators review a 
sample of about 10 of them and the result is recorded. No new screen, table, field, or ongoing review step is 
added.

### Pending Human Decision (relies on an escalated issue)
- In-app notification carries only the country link, the days-since count, and a reason line ('No logged 
engagement for N days; threshold 90; no active mission'). Last engagement date and last contact are not stored
in the notification. They are rendered on the Country page at view time through the existing 
Country/Mission/Engagement permissions. *(waits on C4)*
- Weekly email digest to each recipient lists currently cold countries with country name, days-since band, and
a link into the CRM. It contains no contact names, no engagement dates, and no engagement details. *(waits on 
C4)*
- Recipient resolution happens at send time. Recipients are the currently assigned Regional Coordinators whose
accounts are active and who still hold Country access. If none qualify, the alert goes to existing 
Operations-role holders with access to that country, so the country never goes silently unalerted. Which role 
is the right fallback is an organisational decision for humans. *(waits on C4)*

### Explicitly Excluded from Scope
- Sentiment, relationship-health, or ML scoring of engagement quality
- Per-country, per-region, or per-contact thresholds, seasonality calendars, exclusion lists, and any settings
someone must maintain per country
- Weighting or filtering Engagements by type in the running product, for example mass-mailing detection. This 
is revisited only if the release-gate or post-launch evidence shows masking.
- Dashboards, 'cold countries' lists or queues, job-health screens, and cross-country ranking views
- Snooze, acknowledge, dismiss, approval, or escalation workflows
- Automatic ingestion of email, calendar, or phone activity to infer engagement
- New entities or tables, and new required fields for Regional Coordinators
- Alert delivery to channels outside the CRM other than the weekly digest (no SMS or chat), and to users 
outside existing access roles
- Per-recipient recomputation of last-engagement values based on each recipient's Engagement visibility
- Defining which countries are strategic priorities, what counts as 'cold' diplomatically, the email 
classification policy, and the fallback-recipient policy. These are decisions for humans, not product design.

### Success Criteria
- Every non-suppressed country over the 90-day threshold gets exactly one in-app notification per 30-day 
window. Verified by back-test and by rerunning the nightly job twice on the same day (idempotent, zero 
duplicates).
- After a simulated skipped nightly run, the next run produces all missed notifications with no manual 
intervention.
- Alerts auto-clear within 24 hours of a new Engagement being logged for that country.
- Countries with an active Mission produce zero cold alerts.
- Zero new fields or manual steps are required of Regional Coordinators. The only new schema is the 
auto-filled Country.last_alerted_at column.
- Weekly digest emails contain zero contact names and zero engagement dates (automated test on rendered 
output).
- Delivery test: 100% of cold countries resolve to at least one active recipient with Country access. 
Countries with no assigned active coordinator resolve to the fallback role.
- Day-one back-test: no more than 25% of ~100 countries are flagged, including no-history countries. If the 
share is higher, the threshold is escalated to humans before release.
- Release gate, precision: coordinators confirm a sample of 10 flagged countries as truly cold, and the result
is recorded.
- Release gate, false negatives: coordinators review a sample of about 10 unflagged countries whose most 
recent Engagement is a bulk or multi-recipient record, and the result is recorded. If 3 or more of the 10 are 
judged genuinely cold (the 3-of-10 cutoff is a proposed default for humans to confirm), release is held and 
the threshold and Engagement-type question goes back to humans before launch.
- Weekly digest is delivered to 100% of recipients who have at least one cold country.

## 2. Surfaced Assumptions & Deliberation Outcomes
- **[A1] 'Cold' means no logged Engagement of any type with any contact, mission, or project of the country 
within a single global threshold of 90 days.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C1 (single global threshold) ended ACCEPTED after active-mission suppression and a reason line 
were added. C5 (go-live and edge-trigger semantics) ended ACCEPTED after the move to level-triggered alerting 
with Country.last_alerted_at.
- **[A2] Engagement records already in the CRM are logged as part of normal work and are complete enough to 
act as the activity signal; Regional Coordinators will not be asked to enter anything extra.** (Challenged: 
`True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C2 ended ACCEPTED after the Proposer added a precision gate and a false-negative release gate 
with a 3-of-10 hold rule. In-product Engagement-type filtering stays out of scope.
- **[A3] Each country already has an assigned Regional Coordinator in the existing access model, and alerts go
to that person and respect existing Country/Mission permissions.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: 
`ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN`)
  - *Notes:* C3 (unassigned or departed coordinators) ended ACCEPTED with send-time recipient resolution and 
an Operations-role fallback. C4 (email confidentiality and restricted Engagements) was ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN, so 
the notification, digest, and recipient-resolution scope items stay pending.
- **[A4] In-app notification plus a weekly email digest is sufficient timeliness; real-time alerting is not 
needed because relationships go cold over weeks, not hours.** (Challenged: `False` -> Disposition: `ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* Not challenged. In-app notification plus a weekly digest was accepted as sufficient timeliness.

## 3. Open Questions Requiring Human / Policy Sign-Off
- **[C4] Head of Diplomatic Security (data classification and clearance authority), with Legal; the product 
owner should route this:** Engineering cannot settle the policy on what an alert may reveal. The Proposer 
removed contact names and engagement dates from the email and kept only country name, a days-since band, and a
CRM link. Two points remain. (1) May a country name with a 'cold' label appear in an email that leaves the 
CRM's access-controlled boundary? If not, the digest becomes link-only with no country names. (2) May 
restricted Engagements count toward 'warm' in the system-run job, even though the days-since figure can then 
reflect an Engagement the recipient cannot see? Please also confirm that the fallback recipient role, 
currently Operations-role holders with country access, is the right one.
  - *Why it is here:* The Critic escalated it as a policy question outside engineering's authority.

## 4. Issue Ledger (computed by the engine)
| Issue | Assumption | Proposer moves | Rounds challenged | Final state | Challenge |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | A1 | REVISED | 1 | `ACCEPTED` | A single global 90-day threshold treats Norway and a country with one 
biannual ministerial cycle identically. Countries with seasonal or low-frequency diplomatic rhythms 
(parliamentary recess, elections, summit-driven cadence) will be flagged cold every cycle while being 
perfectly healthy. Regional Coordinators will quickly learn these are noise and start ignoring the digest, so 
the one genuinely cold high-priority country gets lost in the same pile. The 25% day-one cap still allows ~25 
flagged countries, which is already a fatigue-level volume for a coordinator portfolio of perhaps 10-15 
countries. Whether 90 days is the right definition of cold is a policy question, but there must be a bounded 
engineering mitigation, such as a coordinator-visible reason or an exclusion for countries in an active 
mission. |
| C2 | A2 | DEFENDED -> REVISED | 2 | `ACCEPTED` | The signal is last logged Engagement, but logging is 
voluntary and uneven. A coordinator who is in weekly phone contact with a ministry but has not logged it will 
trigger a false 'cold' alert, while a country where an intern bulk-logged a mass-email Engagement looks warm. 
Turnover compounds this: when a coordinator leaves or a country is reassigned, the backlog of unlogged contact
makes the countries look cold exactly when handover is weakest. Also, 'any Engagement of any type' lets 
low-value records (automated or mass-distribution mailings, a logged circular) reset the clock, hiding a truly
cold relationship. The 'complete enough' assumption is untested; the day-one back-test only measures how many 
are flagged, not whether those flags are true. |
| C3 | A3 | REVISED | 1 | `ACCEPTED` | Assumes every country has a current, correct Regional Coordinator. With
turnover across 100 countries, some countries will have no coordinator, a departed one (account deactivated), 
or a stale assignment. The alert then goes to nobody or to the wrong person, and the country silently stays 
cold with no fallback; the exact failure the feature exists to prevent. In the other direction, a former 
coordinator who still holds Country access could keep receiving emails with last-contact names for a country 
they no longer work on. The proposal has no handling for unassigned or inactive recipients. |
| C4 | A3 | REVISED | 1 | `ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN` | The alert and the weekly email include the name of the last 
contact engaged. Email digests leave the CRM's access-controlled boundary: they sit in mailboxes, are 
forwarded, are synced to phones, and persist after a user's CRM access is revoked. 'Inherits Country/Mission 
access' only holds at render time in-app, not for an email sent at 06:00 that is read later. Contact names 
tied to a country and a 'relationship cold' label are diplomatically sensitive. Also, Engagements may be 
restricted by mission or classification, so if last_engagement_date or last contact is derived from a 
restricted Engagement, the alert can leak the existence and the contact of an engagement the recipient cannot 
see in the CRM. The nightly job computes across all engagements, so it is unclear whether it runs with the 
recipient's permissions. |
| C5 | A1 | REVISED | 1 | `ACCEPTED` | The go-live behavior and the transition semantics are ill-defined. 
Countries with no Engagement history are flagged once at go-live; for a CRM that has not been logging 
engagement consistently, this can dump a large batch of labeled-cold alerts onto coordinators on day one, and 
'flagged once' then never re-evaluates them if they are never engaged. Also, 'cold' is an edge-triggered 
notification, and the re-reminder is only through the digest every 30 days. A job failure or a skipped nightly
run could silently miss the crossing, and there is no detection of job failure. The 'exactly one notification'
criterion is untestable without idempotency and missed-run handling. |

